,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326318 1113 #uuid "a00f14bc-7f15-569a-bc33-e10f7eaf55f0" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326318 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/b829ff7e7a51ed9f8089b0b8fc22f727ccf5dc7b2e937ec90f6a1affb8d29064.png)" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326320 13194139535007 true] [17592186326319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326321 13194139535007 true] [17592186326319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326322 13194139535007 true] [17592186326319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326323 13194139535007 true] [17592186326319 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326324 13194139535007 true] [17592186326319 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535007 true] [17592186326319 1041 "selection" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326319 1104 #edb/ref 17592186326322 13194139535007 true] [17592186326319 1110 #uuid "01b7eadf-c003-5ebd-b5a5-951ed22edb4d" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326320 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_A