47173/assets/930509fb9224152be07ef3e14550e0f7afe803428a357963eb3796e0650685a3.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326325 1113 #uuid "29377ed0-f103-5d84-ae34-1182af962bec" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326325 1145 "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the second and third quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/ed4c3e91d019f95cdbb69df855a01b744b7ca016558e5ec67a8fe5efa6d02c59.png)" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326327 13194139535007 true] [17592186326326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326328 13194139535007 true] [17592186326326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326329 13194139535007 true] [17592186326326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326330 13194139535007 true] [17592186326326 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326331 13194139535007 true] [17592186326326 1040 #edb/