32 1017 #edb/ref 17592186326333 13194139535007 true] [17592186326332 1089 "q-160951" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326332 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535007 true] [17592186326332 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/81ccd8830bcafa56065d5f7481e0d0b1dc114d0cd67a9d04220c8bb4459968d3.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326332 1113 #uuid "4857ee0f-58dd-5287-9162-0826380416b7" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326332 1145 "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=0$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14047173/assets/968e682dbaa9d8bdb95808e609a1dba6c68e8e12d9cae1abb59755b5ec640286.png)" 13194139535007 true] [17592186326333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326334 13194139535007 true] [17592186326333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326335 13194139535007 true] [17592186326333 1000 #edb/ref 17592186326336 1